    {
      "question": "e-7312",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "797e41afecb208538dd12929094cec61768eda738efd9fe4a886fe8b49aa3ce1",
      "new_sha256": "d538853af75dc49fc9e128b8d66f9e6cd9af347fd6aaa4b0176a78e939d00b9f",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-7312",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, notice that the right-hand side of the equation is *negative*, which means that the car's value is *decreasing.*\n\nThis is an exponential decay differential equation with $r = 0.1.$ Therefore, the general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 e^{-0.1 t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the car was first purchased for $\\[math]10\\,000.$ Therefore, $V_0 = 10\\,000,$ and we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nV(t) = 10\\,000 e^{-0.1 t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the car's value after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the particular solution above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =10\\,000{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =10\\,000{e}^{-0.3} \\\\  & \u22487\\,408.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car will be worth approximately $\\[/math]7\\,408$ after three years.",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037533/example-7312.json",
          "file_sha256": "8e1d9a5d68f9ef6ab12efb437bb6a5d121eafb8e54b9d9f4019c1a949c4eacb3"
        }
